WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.328
Rosa earns 6,000 dollars a month.

00:00:02.328 --> 00:00:06.207
Her disability policy pays a stated monthly indemnity, a flat

00:00:06.207 --> 00:00:10.086
amount chosen at purchase rather than a reimbursement of bills.

00:00:10.086 --> 00:00:15.129
Insurers cap the benefit at a percentage of earned income, commonly 60 to

00:00:15.129 --> 00:00:20.172
70 percent, so that disability never pays better than work; because individually paid

00:00:20.172 --> 00:00:24.439
benefits are tax-free, 60 percent of gross often approaches take-home pay.

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:25.991
Rosa chooses 3,600 dollars.

00:00:25.991 --> 00:00:29.482
The benefit is fixed unless a rider adjusts it,

00:00:29.482 --> 00:00:32.973
which is why the riders in Lesson 31 exist.

00:00:32.973 --> 00:00:36.077
On the exam, the monthly benefit is the

00:00:36.077 --> 00:00:39.180
amount, the definition decides whether it is owed.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.652
The elimination period is the time deductible: the number

00:00:42.652 --> 00:00:45.739
of days after disability begins before benefits start.

00:00:45.739 --> 00:00:49.597
Common choices are 30, 60, 90, 180 or 365 days.

00:00:49.597 --> 00:00:54.998
A longer elimination period lowers the premium because short disabilities never produce a claim.

00:00:54.998 --> 00:00:58.085
Rosa picks 90 days, planning to live on

00:00:58.085 --> 00:01:00.785
savings and sick pay in the meantime.

00:01:00.785 --> 00:01:05.801
Benefits are then paid monthly in arrears, so her first check arrives about

00:01:05.801 --> 00:01:10.430
a month after day 90, roughly four months after she stopped work.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:15.060
Some policies apply a shorter elimination period to accidents than to sickness.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:19.499
The benefit period is how long payments continue for one disability: two years,

00:01:19.499 --> 00:01:23.938
five years, to age 65 or 67, or for life in older policies.

00:01:23.938 --> 00:01:25.645
Longer benefit periods cost more.

00:01:25.645 --> 00:01:29.401
For a career-ending injury the difference between a five-year benefit period

00:01:29.401 --> 00:01:33.157
and one to age 65 is the whole point of the

00:01:33.157 --> 00:01:36.913
coverage, so Theo recommends the longer period for Rosa at 41.

00:01:36.913 --> 00:01:39.645
Benefit periods for sickness and accident may differ.

00:01:39.645 --> 00:01:43.742
Group plans often define a short-term benefit period in weeks and a

00:01:43.742 --> 00:01:47.840
long-term period to retirement age, handing off from one to the other.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.836
Once Rosa has been disabled for a stated period, usually 90 days, the waiver

00:01:52.836 --> 00:01:57.832
of premium provision excuses her premiums for as long as the disability lasts, and

00:01:57.832 --> 00:02:02.114
the insurer refunds any premium she paid during those first 90 days.

00:02:02.114 --> 00:02:05.326
Coverage stays fully in force while she pays nothing.

00:02:05.326 --> 00:02:07.110
When she recovers, premiums resume.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:11.036
Without the waiver, a disabled insured with no income might let

00:02:11.036 --> 00:02:14.604
the policy lapse at the moment she needs it most.

00:02:14.604 --> 00:02:19.600
Most individual disability policies include the waiver automatically; others sell it as a rider.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.867
Does the policy cover an injury at work?

00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:26.134
Nonoccupational coverage excludes disabilities arising from employment, leaving

00:02:26.134 --> 00:02:28.993
those to workers compensation, and costs less.

00:02:28.993 --> 00:02:33.485
Occupational, or 24-hour, coverage pays whether the disability began on or

00:02:33.485 --> 00:02:37.978
off the job, usually with an offset for workers compensation benefits.

00:02:37.978 --> 00:02:42.878
Group plans are typically nonoccupational because the employer already carries workers compensation.

00:02:42.878 --> 00:02:46.145
Rosa, self-employed and not covered by workers compensation

00:02:46.145 --> 00:02:49.004
unless she buys it, needs 24-hour coverage.

00:02:49.004 --> 00:02:52.271
The question to ask a client is who

00:02:52.271 --> 00:02:55.130
pays if the injury happens at work.

00:02:55.130 --> 00:03:00.984
Disability policies exclude losses from war or military service, intentionally self-inflicted

00:03:00.984 --> 00:03:06.839
injury, participation in a felony, pre-existing conditions during the look-back period,

00:03:06.839 --> 00:03:11.629
and in nonoccupational policies, injuries covered by workers compensation.

00:03:11.629 --> 00:03:18.016
Older policies excluded normal pregnancy; modern policies treat pregnancy complications as sickness.

00:03:18.016 --> 00:03:22.273
Some exclude disability while living outside the country

00:03:22.273 --> 00:03:25.999
for an extended period or while incarcerated.

00:03:25.999 --> 00:03:32.918
Put the settings together for Rosa: 3,600 dollars a month, 90-day elimination, benefits

00:03:32.918 --> 00:03:39.837
to 65, premium waived after 90 days of disability, 24-hour coverage, standard exclusions.

00:03:39.837 --> 00:03:43.030
That is a complete basic plan.
